1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:00,946
Friday,

2
00:00:00,946 --> 00:00:01,967
four forty five.

3
00:00:01,967 --> 00:00:04,836
The build is green on the developer's laptop,

4
00:00:04,836 --> 00:00:06,431
so it goes to production.

5
00:00:06,981 --> 00:00:07,934
Monday morning,

6
00:00:07,934 --> 00:00:09,270
production is broken.

7
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:10,605
The code is the same.

8
00:00:10,605 --> 00:00:12,448
But the build was made again,

9
00:00:12,448 --> 00:00:13,656
on another machine,

10
00:00:13,656 --> 00:00:14,801
with another Java,

11
00:00:14,801 --> 00:00:16,645
and another set of downloads.

12
00:00:17,195 --> 00:00:19,947
Same code. Different build. Different result.

13
00:00:20,497 --> 00:00:23,378
Professional teams solve this with one rule.

14
00:00:23,378 --> 00:00:24,099
Build once.

15
00:00:24,099 --> 00:00:26,129
Then move that exact same file,

16
00:00:26,129 --> 00:00:28,225
from environment to environment,

17
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:29,862
and never build it again.

18
00:00:30,412 --> 00:00:32,339
Today we build that pipeline,

19
00:00:32,339 --> 00:00:32,937
for real.

20
00:00:32,937 --> 00:00:34,067
A commit goes in.

21
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:34,665
A tested,

22
00:00:34,665 --> 00:00:35,329
versioned,

23
00:00:35,329 --> 00:00:37,123
checksummed file comes out,

24
00:00:37,123 --> 00:00:38,718
and it travels from dev,

25
00:00:38,718 --> 00:00:39,316
to stage,

26
00:00:39,316 --> 00:00:40,247
to production.

27
00:00:41,797 --> 00:00:43,226
Here is what to expect.

28
00:00:43,776 --> 00:00:46,073
One: the whole journey on one picture,

29
00:00:46,073 --> 00:00:47,645
from commit to production,

30
00:00:47,645 --> 00:00:49,216
and why each stage exists.

31
00:00:49,766 --> 00:00:52,604
Two: the golden rule. Build once, promote many.

32
00:00:53,154 --> 00:00:55,632
Three: a live demo in real Jenkins,

33
00:00:55,632 --> 00:00:56,411
real Nexus,

34
00:00:56,411 --> 00:00:58,535
and three real Tomcat servers.

35
00:00:58,535 --> 00:00:59,173
We build,

36
00:00:59,173 --> 00:00:59,952
we publish,

37
00:00:59,952 --> 00:01:01,155
we deploy to dev,

38
00:01:01,155 --> 00:01:04,908
and we promote the same file to stage and production.

39
00:01:05,458 --> 00:01:07,222
Four: we ship a second version,

40
00:01:07,222 --> 00:01:10,578
and we see why production can safely stay on the first one.

41
00:01:11,128 --> 00:01:16,312
This lesson covers the first three objectives of the CI CD Engineering domain.

42
00:01:17,862 --> 00:01:23,281
The journey, on one picture. It starts with a commit. A developer saves a change in Git.

43
00:01:23,831 --> 00:01:29,185
Stage one: build. The code is compiled, on a clean machine, the same way every time.

44
00:01:29,735 --> 00:01:35,431
Stage two: test. If one test fails, the line stops here. Nothing broken moves forward.

45
00:01:35,981 --> 00:01:40,739
Stage three: package. The result is one file. For Cloud Cart, a war file.

46
00:01:41,289 --> 00:01:42,634
Stage four: publish.

47
00:01:42,634 --> 00:01:44,652
The file goes to a repository,

48
00:01:44,652 --> 00:01:45,392
here Nexus,

49
00:01:45,392 --> 00:01:47,881
with a version number and a checksum.

50
00:01:48,431 --> 00:01:54,297
Stage five: deploy. The file goes to a server. First to dev, where developers look at it.

51
00:01:54,847 --> 00:01:56,046
Stage six: verify.

52
00:01:56,046 --> 00:01:58,178
Do not assume the deploy worked.

53
00:01:58,178 --> 00:02:01,375
Ask the running application which version it is.

54
00:02:01,925 --> 00:02:03,267
Why separate stages?

55
00:02:03,267 --> 00:02:05,279
Because when something is red,

56
00:02:05,279 --> 00:02:07,559
the stage tells you where to look.

57
00:02:07,559 --> 00:02:09,101
Red at test means code.

58
00:02:09,101 --> 00:02:11,516
Red at publish means the repository.

59
00:02:11,516 --> 00:02:13,595
Red at verify means the server.

60
00:02:15,145 --> 00:02:17,918
Now the golden rule. Build once, promote many.

61
00:02:18,468 --> 00:02:20,267
The wrong way: build for dev,

62
00:02:20,267 --> 00:02:21,632
build again for stage,

63
00:02:21,632 --> 00:02:23,307
build again for production.

64
00:02:23,307 --> 00:02:25,726
Three builds are three different files,

65
00:02:25,726 --> 00:02:27,215
even from the same code.

66
00:02:27,765 --> 00:02:29,740
The right way: build one time.

67
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:31,847
Give that file a unique version.

68
00:02:31,847 --> 00:02:33,032
Store it in Nexus.

69
00:02:33,032 --> 00:02:36,192
Then every environment downloads that same file.

70
00:02:36,742 --> 00:02:38,919
Two protections make this safe.

71
00:02:38,919 --> 00:02:41,096
One: a version is never reused.

72
00:02:41,096 --> 00:02:44,608
Nexus refuses a second upload of the same version.

73
00:02:44,608 --> 00:02:46,083
That is immutability,

74
00:02:46,083 --> 00:02:47,558
from the last domain.

75
00:02:48,108 --> 00:02:49,193
Two: a checksum.

76
00:02:49,193 --> 00:02:53,193
Each download is compared with the checksum Nexus recorded.

77
00:02:53,193 --> 00:02:54,550
If one byte differs,

78
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:55,702
the deploy stops.

79
00:02:56,252 --> 00:03:00,263
That is promotion. Not a new build. The same file, moving forward.

80
00:03:01,813 --> 00:03:02,724
Real Jenkins,

81
00:03:02,724 --> 00:03:04,617
signed in as administrator.

82
00:03:04,617 --> 00:03:05,248
Two jobs.

83
00:03:05,248 --> 00:03:07,701
Cloud Cart CI builds and publishes.

84
00:03:07,701 --> 00:03:13,098
Cloud Cart promote moves a finished version from one environment to the next.

85
00:03:13,648 --> 00:03:18,299
First, the terminal. This is the Cloud Cart project, and its pipeline files.

86
00:03:18,849 --> 00:03:21,324
Now the Jenkins file. Four stages.

87
00:03:21,874 --> 00:03:22,524
Stage one,

88
00:03:22,524 --> 00:03:23,043
Version.

89
00:03:23,043 --> 00:03:24,863
The version is one point oh,

90
00:03:24,863 --> 00:03:25,123
dot,

91
00:03:25,123 --> 00:03:26,228
the build number.

92
00:03:26,228 --> 00:03:28,177
Every build gets a new number,

93
00:03:28,177 --> 00:03:30,322
and a number is never used twice.

94
00:03:30,872 --> 00:03:36,909
Stage two, Build and test. Stage three, Publish to Nexus. Stage four, Deploy to dev.

95
00:03:37,459 --> 00:03:39,309
Look at the environment block.

96
00:03:39,309 --> 00:03:41,962
The password for Nexus is not in this file.

97
00:03:41,962 --> 00:03:43,010
Jenkins keeps it,

98
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:45,971
and hands it to the pipeline only while it runs.

99
00:03:46,521 --> 00:03:51,897
The settings file for the build asks for the user name and the password from the environment.

100
00:03:51,897 --> 00:03:53,689
Nothing secret is written down.

101
00:03:54,239 --> 00:03:58,079
And this is the deploy script. It downloads the war from Nexus.

102
00:03:58,629 --> 00:04:01,765
It compares the checksum, and stops if it does not match.

103
00:04:02,315 --> 00:04:07,499
Then it copies the file to Tomcat, and asks the running application which version it is.

104
00:04:08,049 --> 00:04:09,558
Before we run anything,

105
00:04:09,558 --> 00:04:10,410
where are we?

106
00:04:10,410 --> 00:04:11,657
Three environments,

107
00:04:11,657 --> 00:04:11,919
dev,

108
00:04:11,919 --> 00:04:13,296
stage and production,

109
00:04:13,296 --> 00:04:14,805
each one a real Tomcat.

110
00:04:14,805 --> 00:04:16,838
We ask each one for its health.

111
00:04:17,388 --> 00:04:20,588
Dev first. Not found. Nothing is deployed yet.

112
00:04:21,138 --> 00:04:22,482
Stage. Not found.

113
00:04:23,932 --> 00:04:26,258
Production. Not found. A clean start.

114
00:04:26,808 --> 00:04:29,709
Back to Jenkins. We open Cloud Cart CI.

115
00:04:33,359 --> 00:04:34,532
And click Build Now.

116
00:04:37,282 --> 00:04:41,016
Jenkins puts the build in the queue, and starts it in a second or two.

117
00:04:43,066 --> 00:04:44,712
This is the Stages view.

118
00:04:44,712 --> 00:04:46,427
Each circle is one stage.

119
00:04:46,427 --> 00:04:47,113
The first,

120
00:04:47,113 --> 00:04:47,730
Checkout,

121
00:04:47,730 --> 00:04:49,719
is Jenkins fetching the code.

122
00:04:49,719 --> 00:04:52,531
The rest are the four stages of our file.

123
00:04:52,531 --> 00:04:53,903
Watch them go green.

124
00:04:54,453 --> 00:04:58,933
Version is done. The pipeline stamped this build, one point oh point one.

125
00:04:59,483 --> 00:05:03,963
Build and test. This compiles the code and runs all thirty two tests.

126
00:05:04,513 --> 00:05:05,649
Publish to Nexus.

127
00:05:05,649 --> 00:05:07,520
The war file leaves Jenkins,

128
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:09,324
and goes to the repository,

129
00:05:09,324 --> 00:05:10,593
with its checksums.

130
00:05:11,143 --> 00:05:12,090
Deploy to dev.

131
00:05:12,090 --> 00:05:14,189
The file comes back from Nexus,

132
00:05:14,189 --> 00:05:15,610
the checksum matches,

133
00:05:15,610 --> 00:05:17,031
and Tomcat starts it.

134
00:05:17,031 --> 00:05:19,941
Notice what happens here: nothing is built.

135
00:05:19,941 --> 00:05:21,701
The deploy only downloads,

136
00:05:21,701 --> 00:05:22,175
checks,

137
00:05:22,175 --> 00:05:22,919
and copies.

138
00:05:29,169 --> 00:05:31,772
Green. Build number one. We open the build.

139
00:05:33,222 --> 00:05:34,608
And the console output.

140
00:05:36,058 --> 00:05:39,322
It is long, because Maven and Jenkins log everything.

141
00:05:40,572 --> 00:05:42,514
Thirty two tests, no failures.

142
00:05:43,064 --> 00:05:44,367
The checksum is ok,

143
00:05:44,367 --> 00:05:48,002
and dev reports it is running one point oh point one.

144
00:05:48,002 --> 00:05:50,402
That last line is the verify stage.

145
00:05:50,952 --> 00:05:54,515
Now Nexus. This is where the file went. We open Browse.

146
00:05:58,165 --> 00:05:59,914
The releases repository.

147
00:06:05,264 --> 00:06:06,288
Then academy.

148
00:06:09,938 --> 00:06:11,069
Then cloud cart.

149
00:06:14,519 --> 00:06:15,479
And the version.

150
00:06:19,129 --> 00:06:20,622
One point oh point one.

151
00:06:23,772 --> 00:06:25,031
Here is the war file.

152
00:06:28,581 --> 00:06:29,707
Next to the war,

153
00:06:29,707 --> 00:06:31,536
Nexus keeps its checksums,

154
00:06:31,536 --> 00:06:32,943
in a S H A one file,

155
00:06:32,943 --> 00:06:34,420
and an M D five file.

156
00:06:34,420 --> 00:06:37,797
This is the number every environment must match.

157
00:06:38,347 --> 00:06:41,248
Back in the terminal. Is dev alive? Health, dev.

158
00:06:42,898 --> 00:06:45,522
Status up, version one point oh point one.

159
00:06:46,072 --> 00:06:47,702
And the real application,

160
00:06:47,702 --> 00:06:48,158
on dev.

161
00:06:48,158 --> 00:06:49,397
This is Cloud Cart,

162
00:06:49,397 --> 00:06:50,831
built by the pipeline,

163
00:06:50,831 --> 00:06:51,939
served by Tomcat.

164
00:06:54,389 --> 00:06:58,250
The banner says free shipping over seventy five dollars. Remember that.

165
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:01,915
Stage and production are still empty. Health, stage.

166
00:07:04,765 --> 00:07:05,896
Still not found.

167
00:07:06,446 --> 00:07:10,179
Time to promote. In Jenkins, we open Cloud Cart promote.

168
00:07:13,729 --> 00:07:15,181
This job has no build,

169
00:07:15,181 --> 00:07:15,775
no tests,

170
00:07:15,775 --> 00:07:16,567
no compiler.

171
00:07:16,567 --> 00:07:19,470
It only moves a version that already exists.

172
00:07:19,470 --> 00:07:21,516
We click Build with Parameters.

173
00:07:24,766 --> 00:07:29,118
Two parameters. Version, and target. We type one point oh point one.

174
00:07:29,668 --> 00:07:31,076
And the target is stage.

175
00:07:31,626 --> 00:07:32,351
Build.

176
00:07:35,001 --> 00:07:35,596
Remember,

177
00:07:35,596 --> 00:07:38,110
this job has no compiler and no tests.

178
00:07:38,110 --> 00:07:39,896
It only downloads the file,

179
00:07:39,896 --> 00:07:40,558
checks it,

180
00:07:40,558 --> 00:07:41,550
and deploys it.

181
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:42,724
One stage,

182
00:07:42,724 --> 00:07:43,223
Promote.

183
00:07:43,223 --> 00:07:45,594
It downloaded the same war from Nexus,

184
00:07:45,594 --> 00:07:46,905
checked the checksum,

185
00:07:46,905 --> 00:07:47,903
and deployed it.

186
00:07:48,453 --> 00:07:51,354
Now production. We open the promote job again.

187
00:07:54,404 --> 00:07:56,090
And click Build with Parameters.

188
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:02,376
Version one point oh point one. Target, prod.

189
00:08:05,126 --> 00:08:05,542
Build.

190
00:08:05,542 --> 00:08:06,168
Same job,

191
00:08:06,168 --> 00:08:06,862
same file,

192
00:08:06,862 --> 00:08:07,766
a new target.

193
00:08:07,766 --> 00:08:10,405
Jenkins downloads it from Nexus again,

194
00:08:10,405 --> 00:08:11,795
checks the checksum,

195
00:08:11,795 --> 00:08:12,837
and deploys it.

196
00:08:12,837 --> 00:08:13,254
Green.

197
00:08:19,304 --> 00:08:21,501
Back in the terminal. Health, stage.

198
00:08:23,451 --> 00:08:26,374
Version one point oh point one. And production.

199
00:08:27,924 --> 00:08:31,806
Also one point oh point one. Three environments, one version.

200
00:08:32,356 --> 00:08:34,183
But is it really the same file?

201
00:08:34,183 --> 00:08:35,832
Not the same version number,

202
00:08:35,832 --> 00:08:36,716
the same bytes.

203
00:08:36,716 --> 00:08:40,015
We compute the checksum of the file in each environment.

204
00:08:40,565 --> 00:08:45,002
Dev. Stage. Production. Look at the first few characters of each line.

205
00:08:45,552 --> 00:08:46,255
Identical.

206
00:08:46,255 --> 00:08:46,958
Same file,

207
00:08:46,958 --> 00:08:47,871
three places.

208
00:08:47,871 --> 00:08:49,838
Nobody built anything again.

209
00:08:49,838 --> 00:08:51,525
That is the golden rule,

210
00:08:51,525 --> 00:08:52,016
proven.

211
00:08:52,566 --> 00:08:53,984
Now the advanced part.

212
00:08:53,984 --> 00:08:55,982
A developer changes the banner.

213
00:08:55,982 --> 00:08:58,689
Free shipping now starts at fifty dollars.

214
00:08:59,239 --> 00:09:01,330
We make the change, with one command.

215
00:09:03,380 --> 00:09:05,428
We look at the change. One line.

216
00:09:05,978 --> 00:09:06,916
And commit it.

217
00:09:10,166 --> 00:09:13,110
Back in Jenkins, we open Cloud Cart CI.

218
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:17,834
And click Build Now.

219
00:09:22,284 --> 00:09:23,967
The pipeline runs again.

220
00:09:23,967 --> 00:09:25,159
Same four stages.

221
00:09:25,159 --> 00:09:25,720
Version,

222
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:27,334
one point oh point two.

223
00:09:27,334 --> 00:09:28,386
Build and test,

224
00:09:28,386 --> 00:09:30,279
all thirty two tests again.

225
00:09:30,279 --> 00:09:30,840
Publish,

226
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:32,944
a new file with a new version.

227
00:09:32,944 --> 00:09:34,207
And deploy to dev.

228
00:09:34,207 --> 00:09:37,644
Notice that stage and production are not touched.

229
00:09:54,494 --> 00:09:55,381
While it runs,

230
00:09:55,381 --> 00:09:57,027
look at the version again.

231
00:09:57,027 --> 00:09:58,991
It comes from the build number.

232
00:09:58,991 --> 00:09:59,625
Build two,

233
00:09:59,625 --> 00:10:01,588
version one point oh point two.

234
00:10:01,588 --> 00:10:02,602
Nobody typed it,

235
00:10:02,602 --> 00:10:04,819
and nobody can forget to change it.

236
00:10:05,369 --> 00:10:06,418
Build number two.

237
00:10:06,418 --> 00:10:08,763
The version is one point oh point two.

238
00:10:08,763 --> 00:10:09,565
A new number,

239
00:10:09,565 --> 00:10:11,108
because it is a new file.

240
00:10:11,658 --> 00:10:13,044
Dev shows the new banner.

241
00:10:19,694 --> 00:10:21,998
Free shipping now starts at fifty dollars.

242
00:10:22,548 --> 00:10:24,185
And stage and production?

243
00:10:24,185 --> 00:10:26,673
They still run one point oh point one.

244
00:10:26,673 --> 00:10:27,852
Dev moved forward.

245
00:10:27,852 --> 00:10:29,095
The others did not,

246
00:10:29,095 --> 00:10:30,143
until we decide.

247
00:10:30,693 --> 00:10:32,442
We decide that stage should get it.

248
00:10:36,892 --> 00:10:39,346
In the promote job, Build with Parameters.

249
00:10:42,596 --> 00:10:45,710
Version one point oh point two. Target, stage.

250
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:46,682
Build.

251
00:10:46,682 --> 00:10:48,858
The same promote job as before.

252
00:10:48,858 --> 00:10:51,737
It finds one point oh point two in Nexus,

253
00:10:51,737 --> 00:10:53,141
checks the checksum,

254
00:10:53,141 --> 00:10:55,178
and deploys it to stage only.

255
00:11:00,928 --> 00:11:04,480
Now the three environments are on different versions,

256
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:05,217
on purpose.

257
00:11:05,217 --> 00:11:07,966
Dev and stage run one point oh point two.

258
00:11:07,966 --> 00:11:10,848
Production stays on one point oh point one.

259
00:11:11,398 --> 00:11:16,134
That is what promotion gives you. Production changes only when someone chooses it.

260
00:11:16,684 --> 00:11:20,716
One more thing. In Nexus, we open the releases repository.

261
00:11:25,366 --> 00:11:26,539
First, academy.

262
00:11:29,989 --> 00:11:31,120
Then cloud cart.

263
00:11:34,670 --> 00:11:36,014
And cloud cart again.

264
00:11:39,464 --> 00:11:40,963
Both versions are there.

265
00:11:40,963 --> 00:11:42,400
One point oh point one,

266
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:44,087
and one point oh point two.

267
00:11:44,087 --> 00:11:45,586
Nothing was overwritten.

268
00:11:46,136 --> 00:11:49,803
Could someone upload a different file as one point oh point one?

269
00:11:49,803 --> 00:11:50,204
We try,

270
00:11:50,204 --> 00:11:51,235
from the terminal.

271
00:11:55,385 --> 00:11:56,298
Four oh nine.

272
00:11:56,298 --> 00:11:56,930
Conflict.

273
00:11:56,930 --> 00:11:57,913
Nexus refuses.

274
00:11:57,913 --> 00:12:00,510
A released version cannot be changed,

275
00:12:00,510 --> 00:12:03,038
so production can always be rebuilt,

276
00:12:03,038 --> 00:12:04,091
or rolled back,

277
00:12:04,091 --> 00:12:06,478
from the exact file it ran before.

278
00:12:08,028 --> 00:12:09,031
Let us recap.

279
00:12:09,581 --> 00:12:11,073
A pipeline is stages.

280
00:12:11,073 --> 00:12:11,499
Build,

281
00:12:11,499 --> 00:12:11,854
test,

282
00:12:11,854 --> 00:12:12,423
package,

283
00:12:12,423 --> 00:12:12,991
publish,

284
00:12:12,991 --> 00:12:14,270
deploy and verify.

285
00:12:14,270 --> 00:12:17,965
Each one answers a different question when it fails.

286
00:12:18,515 --> 00:12:23,123
Build once. Give the file a unique version, and keep it in a repository.

287
00:12:23,673 --> 00:12:29,006
Promote the same file. Dev, then stage, then production. Check the checksum every time.

288
00:12:29,556 --> 00:12:34,121
And verify by asking the running application, not by trusting the deploy.

289
00:12:34,671 --> 00:12:40,026
Next lesson: the gates that stop a bad build, secrets, and safe deploys with rollback.

290
00:12:41,576 --> 00:12:43,667
Your turn. On your own machine only.

291
00:12:44,217 --> 00:12:49,977
One. Write a Jenkins file with four stages. Version, build and test, publish, deploy.

292
00:12:50,527 --> 00:12:53,129
Two. Give every build a version from the build number.

293
00:12:53,679 --> 00:12:58,629
Three. Make a second job that takes a version and a target, and deploys without building.

294
00:12:59,179 --> 00:13:03,232
Four. Prove it: compute the checksum in two environments, and compare.

295
00:13:03,782 --> 00:13:06,363
Pause the video, and do it before you continue.

296
00:13:21,913 --> 00:13:23,556
Quick check. Answer aloud.

297
00:13:24,106 --> 00:13:25,748
Why do we build only once?

298
00:13:26,298 --> 00:13:29,178
What does promotion change, and what does it not change?

299
00:13:29,728 --> 00:13:33,163
What stops someone replacing version one point oh point one?

300
00:13:33,713 --> 00:13:36,017
Why verify by asking the application?

301
